   153	He had packed his own box nineteen times. That was the thing nobody at the table knew about him, and it had come into his head while the big one was naming his shirts. A posting ended on a morning like any other; a letter came; a man put what he owned into one box and his papers into another and went down to the coach. He knew to the ounce what a life weighed when it had to be carried. He knew, too, that the box you packed was never the problem. It was the other one, the one that would not go in a box at all, and he had watched the big one put his hand flat over it and say so, and nobody at the table had asked, and Seln had liked them all very much for that.
   154	
   155	The dairy step, an hour ago, with the lamp in his hand, and the four of them coming up past him into the dark one at a time. The big one with a chair on his shoulder and a letter he would not open. The researcher, who had drawn the corridor they were all standing in more truly than anyone in the registry could have drawn it, and did not know who had built the walls. The girl with the chalk, who had walked a road east in three pieces so that nobody would see her add it up. The boy last, with his receipts. He had lit each of them up one step. He had not told one of them what the step was made of.
   156	
   157	---
   158	
   159	There was a cupboard under the copying table, and he opened it without looking, because his hand knew where the latch was.
   160	
   161	Nineteen bundles stood in it on their ends, tied with the office's grey tape, each one his own duplicates from one posting. He had never kept a list of them. He had never needed to. The labels told him: a town's reference, a year, a count of subjects in his small square hand. One academy's label said four. A district seat's said eleven. The newest, on the end of the row, said one, and had the name of this hill on it. Fifteen years of his trade stood in that cupboard on their ends, sixty-three subjects in all, and every page in every bundle was as true and as empty as the return on the table.
   162	
   163	He had been proud of that once. It was in a commendation, in a drawer, somewhere.
   164	
   165	The newest bundle was the thickest. He did not take it out. He knew what was in it, and he knew, besides, what was not in it, which was more.
   166	
   167	Not in it: a document table in the mill-town hall, a year ago, where he had told the boy that his figures were too tidy, and watched the boy go away and come back with a season of figures that never once settled into a line. Not in it: two slips of wing paper, a year apart, which the boy had read and put on the common-room coals without a word to anyone, which was exactly right. Not in it: Norhold, where everything the boy had kept back for a whole season went out of him in a single afternoon, in front of eight thousand people, on purpose. Seln had sat in that stand and watched it go and understood that the boy had chosen the day. He had never asked him why. He did not ask subjects why.
   168	
   169	The boy believed the instrument had a fault in it, here and there, that a patient reader could find and read back into true. Tonight he had laid out his whole case for it, every piece, in order and then out of order, at full strength. It was the best argument Seln had heard from anybody in years. Every piece of it was true. And somewhere in the registry, in the same file as every one of those pieces, Seln's own returns lay too, true among true things, and the boy would have counted them as receipts.
   170	
   171	He reached up, once, while the cupboard stood open, and put his hand on the edge of the shelf above the copying table, in the dark, where the other thing stood. He did not take it down. He left his hand on the shelf's edge for the space of a long breath, the way a man rests his hand on a door he has decided not to open tonight, and took it away.
   172	
   173	He shut the cupboard.
   174	
   175	The last thing he let himself think about, because it was the oldest, was a press.
   176	
   177	His fourth posting, twelve years ago. A records room in an old building, which he had been in lawfully, looking for somebody's travel endorsements; and a press he had opened for that reason, and found to hold something else. *Maintenance records*, the label said, and that was what they looked like: a long run of upkeep, routine, dated, initialled, older than the building. He had copied a quantity of them before he went, because he copied things. It was not suspicion. It was the oldest habit he had. As a boy he had copied his mother's ledgers for the pleasure of seeing the two hands come out the same.
   178	
   179	He had not understood them for most of a year. The understanding had come all at once in a corridor distempered green to the shoulder, between one ordinary appointment and the next, the way a column of figures will sometimes add itself while you are looking out of a window. He had stood still in that corridor long enough for a clerk to come out of a side door and ask if he was unwell. He had said no. He had kept his appointment, and kept it well.
   180	
   181	And he had known from that day that every question his trade asked about a subject was the wrong question. The files asked whether a person fit. None of them, ever, asked whose rule the fitting was measured against, or how long it had been in use, or by whom. He had paper that showed whose question it had been all along. As far as he knew, nobody else alive had.
   182	
   183	He had not left the service. A man in his trade could not leave it and keep what he kept. So he had stayed, and filed true, and kept.
   184	
   185	The fire-watch climbed down off his bucket at the turn of the hour, and the square of light on the counter slid sideways and went out. Seln sat on in the dark with the key still on his palm. Before he put it away he said one sentence to himself, in the voice he kept for the audit, which was the only voice in himself he believed, so that it would be on a record somewhere even if the only record was in his head.
   186	
   187	*For fifteen years I made the same decision every morning and never said it aloud, and it was quiet. The deciding has stopped being quiet.*

